course: dictionary
lesson: 12402
title: Curtsey: a polite bow
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A curtsey is a polite movement that shows respect. You can work out its meaning from the context: when a girl meets a queen, she does something special to show she respects her. A curtsey can be a noun (a curtsey) or a verb (to curtsey).
explain: Look at the clues: «Lily curtseyed to the Queen before she sat down.» The Queen is important, and Lily shows respect. Try swapping in "said hello": it does not fit, because the word is about showing respect in a special way.
explain: Spelling can vary. Curtsy is also correct, and curtsied is the past tense. Alice in Wonderland even tries to curtsey while she is falling down a rabbit hole, so you can see curtseying is an action people do with their whole body.
fact: A curtsey is a polite movement: you bend your knees with one foot behind the other.
fact: Girls and women curtsey to show respect, for example to a queen.
fact: Dancers curtsey at the end of a performance to thank the audience.
fact: In royal etiquette, women curtsey and men bow to the monarch.
fact: «Curtsey», «curtsy», «curtsied» and «curtseying» are all spellings you may see in books.
exercise: «The girl made a neat curtsey to the Queen.» What is a curtsey? | A way of running very fast | *A polite movement, bending the knees, to show respect | A kind of hat | A loud shout of joy
exercise: Where do dancers often curtsey? | *At the end of a performance | In the middle of a football match | Under the water | At the start of a race
exercise: «Mia curtseyed to the King.» What tense is curtseyed? | *Past tense: it happened before now | Plural: more than one thing | A noun, a thing | Future tense: it will happen later
test: Why do girls curtsey to a queen? | To make a loud noise | To win a race | *To show respect | To leave the room quickly
test: Which spelling is also correct for curtsey? | Cursey | Curtsay | Curtain | *Curtsy
exam: Which sentence uses curtsey as a noun? | She will curtsey before the judges tomorrow. | *She made a deep curtsey before the judges. | She is curtseying before the judges now. | She curtseyed before the judges.
